question: 39, answer 163 {'source': 'Answer the following question given this paragraph: \n\n\n\n\nQ: Each daughter cell represents one outcome of all possible combinations of maternal and paternal what?\n\n Choices:\n\n- traits\n\n- alleles\n\n- genes\n\n- chromosomes\n\nA:', 'target': 'chromosomes', 'rationale': 'The paragraph describes the process of fertilization, which occurs when a sperm cell and egg unite to form an embryo. For each new generation, alleles are combined randomly through meiosis so that half of the chromosomes come from one parent and half from the other. It is important to note that it does not say traits or genes because these can be multiple combinations for any single allele since there could be more than 2 copies per chromosome (e.g., if we use sickle-cell disease as an example).\n\nSo what this question really means by "all possible combinations" refers to all 23 pairs in either maternal or paternal cells giving you 46 total chromosomes during fertilization/meiosis I division when homologous pairs separate into different daughter cells after crossing over has occurred between two sister chrom', 'task': 'sciq', 'type': 'CoT'}
