question: 44, answer 113 {'source': 'Sandbars are a part of most seas because there are\n\nPick the right answer from the list:\n- waves which adjust silt\n- lots of stones in seas\n- places where fish eat\n- sand that needs to be piled', 'target': 'waves which adjust silt', 'rationale': 'Sandbars are underwater features that form in shallow water near shore, usually as a result of the back and forth motion of waves. The movement of these waves carries sediment from one place to another, depositing it in piles on the seafloor. These accumulations can become large enough for their tops to be above sea level at low tide, forming sandbars or even islands if they grow big enough. So most seas have sandbanks because there are currents which move silt around like shifting sands - answer: "waves which adjust silt".', 'task': 'openbookqa', 'type': 'CoT'}
