question: 141, answer 162 {'source': 'Question: "What interpretation becomes a religious requirement?"\n\nContext: "However, the Orthodox claim to absolute fidelity to past tradition has been challenged by scholars who contend that the Judaism of the Middle Ages bore little resemblance to that practiced by today\'s Orthodox. Rather, the Orthodox community, as a counterreaction to the liberalism of the Haskalah movement, began to embrace far more stringent halachic practices than their predecessors, most notably in matters of Kashrut and Passover dietary laws, where the strictest possible interpretation becomes a religious requirement, even where the Talmud explicitly prefers a more lenient position, and even where a more lenient position was practiced by prior generations."\n\nAnswer:', 'target': 'Kashrut and Passover dietary laws', 'rationale': 'The context of the question is about how Orthodox Jews practice and interpret their religion. The answer to the question "what interpretation becomes a religious requirement?" can be found in this sentence:\n"Rather, the Orthodox community, as a counterreaction to the liberalism of the Haskalah movement, began to embrace far more stringent halachic practices than their predecessors, most notably in matters of Kashrut and Passover dietary laws..."\nKashrut are Jewish dietary laws that define what foods may or may not be eaten by observant Jews. Passover (or Pesach) commemorates Israel\'s Exodus from Egypt under Moses\' leadership nearly 3 millennia ago. This holiday lasts for eight days during which no leavened bread products are consumed - hence kosher-for-passover', 'task': 'adversarial_qa@droberta', 'type': 'CoT'}
