question: 160, answer 162 {'source': "Article: Beginning as a self-help program in the mid-1960s, the Cotton Research and Promotion Program (CRPP) was organized by U.S. cotton producers in response to cotton's steady decline in market share. At that time, producers voted to set up a per-bale assessment system to fund the program, with built-in safeguards to protect their investments. With the passage of the Cotton Research and Promotion Act of 1966, the program joined forces and began battling synthetic competitors and re-establishing markets for cotton. Today, the success of this program has made cotton the best-selling fiber in the U.S. and one of the best-selling fibers in the world.[citation needed]\n\nQuestion: What prompted a help program produced by cotton producers in the 1960s?", 'target': 'decline in market share', 'rationale': 'The context states that the program was organized by U.S. cotton producers in response to a decline in market share of cotton, which prompted them to set up a per-bale assessment system for funding the self-help program.\nThere are other words in this paragraph such as "investments" and "safeguards", but these refer not specifically to what precipitated the help program\'s creation; rather they seem like steps taken after its inception (and likely before passage of 1966 Act). Of course there is no indication how long it took between initial idea and completion/passage, so we cannot say with certainty whether investments or safeguards preceded or followed decline in market share (though one might argue that if you\'re setting up an ongoing fund then you should probably consider protections from outside forces', 'task': 'squad_v1', 'type': 'CoT'}
