question: 24, answer 155 {'source': 'Q: Interconnected pathways through which carbon is recycled through the biotic and abiotic components of what?\n\n\nA:', 'target': 'biosphere', 'rationale': 'The biosphere is everything on Earth that supports life. It includes the atmosphere, hydrosphere and lithosphere as well as all of their inhabitants (plants, animals and other living organisms). Carbon is an essential component for life; it makes up more than half of our bodies. \nCarbon recycling refers to how carbon circulates through both organic matter and inorganic matter like minerals or water. For example: when a plant uses sunlight during photosynthesis to convert carbon dioxide into glucose, then dies and decomposes back into the soil. When this happens, the decomposed material provides nutrients needed by plants so they can grow again and provide food energy for humans once eaten. Soil microorganisms also play an important role in helping keep these cycles running smoothly!', 'task': 'sciq', 'type': 'CoT'}
