question: 185, answer 212 {'source': "In this task, you're given the title and three arbitrary sentences out of a five-sentence story. You are also given three additional sentence options, a, b, and c, that may or may not belong to the story. Your job is to pick the two options that seamlessly connect with the rest of the story; note that the selected choices may fit into the story at any point. Indicate your selected choices in order, i.e., as 'ab'/'bc'/'ac' and not as 'ba'/'ca'/'cb'. If options are equally plausible, pick the ones that make more sense.\n\nTitle: The police investigated the man. He wondered who they were following. The man was later found guilty. Choices: a. They followed him for a little bit. b. Miles made a jazz record. c. They could not find anything illegal.", 'target': 'ac', 'rationale': 'The story starts with the police investigating a man (Sentence1), and later it\'s found that the man is guilty (Sentence3). So, it is plausible to assume that the police managed to find something illegal about the man. The second sentence tells us that "He wondered who they were following". This implies that there are multiple people being followed by the police. Who could those people be? The choice c provides an answer "They could not find anything illegal." This means that there are other people besides this particular guy who were being investigated. But in this case, we don\'t know if these others are found guilty or not. Choice a says \\"They followed him for a little bit\\", which makes sense because they did investigate him and he was later found guilty as stated in Sentence3 of the story. Since choices c and a suit with each other as well as with Sentences1, 2, 3 of our story, we choose them both to complete our story ending i.e., \'ac\'.', 'task': 'roc_stories', 'type': 'CoT'}
