question: 47, answer 162 {'source': 'Here\'s a logic test: A tree trunk is thicker then a tree leaf. Which part of the tree is the stronger one? (A) leaf (B) trunk\n\nChoose the answer between "trunk" and "leaf".', 'target': 'leaf', 'rationale': 'The question asks which of two things is stronger, A or B. However, if thing B is thicker than thing A it does not necessarily follow that thing B is stronger than thing A. In fact, the thickness of a leaf has no bearing on how strong it can be relative to other objects. Therefore we should answer according to what else we know about object strength in general rather than just by comparing these two items based solely on their respective thicknesses.\nFor example: If I had photo copies (B) and original documents (A), it would usually make sense for me to say that "the copies are thicker" because they have been printed on both sides while originals are single-sided sheets... but this still doesn\'t mean my copies could withstand more pressure/weight in comparison with my', 'task': 'quarel', 'type': 'CoT'}
