question: 61, answer 155 {'source': 'Two layers of sedimentary rock are exposed on the side of a hill. Only one of the layers has fossils. The lack of fossils in one layer of sedimentary rock is most likely due to\n\nOptions:\n- changing environments.\n- volcanic activity.\n- changing rates of erosion.\n- evaporation of sea water.', 'target': 'changing environments.', 'rationale': "Two layers of sedimentary rock are exposed on the side of a hill. Only one layer has fossils, so the lack of fossils in one layer is most likely due to changing environments.\nIf volcanic activity were responsible for this kind of fossil distribution, we would expect to see different kinds and ages of rocks nearby that would have been deposited by volcanism between deposition periods (layers) 1 and 2 which do not appear to be present here. \nWe can also rule out evaporation because it does not explain why only some organisms became fossilized while others did not; if evaporated material formed sedimentary rock then all organisms found at any time during sea water's exposure should become trapped within the resulting sediments regardless if they died or migrated away from them later on before these areas/", 'task': 'ai2_arc@ARC-Challenge', 'type': 'CoT'}
