question: 65, answer 102 {'source': 'Here is a premise:\nPeople travel to weddings all the time.\n\nHere is a hypothesis:\nIt is very rare for people to travel to weddings.\n\nHere are the options: OPTIONS:\n- yes\n- it is not possible to tell\n- no\nIs it possible to conclude that if the premise is true, then so is the hypothesis?', 'target': 'no', 'rationale': 'The premise states that it is a common occurrence for people to travel to weddings. However, the hypothesis describes this as a rare event instead of a common one. So if the premise were true then the opposite would also be true (i.e., "it\'s not very rare for people"). Therefore, we cannot conclude that the statement made in our options would hold up because if one thing is false then its converse must necessarily be true; hence why choosing \'no\' was correct here!', 'task': 'mnli', 'type': 'CoT'}
