question: 144, answer 162 {'source': 'Q: Unlike other tetrapod vertebrates (reptiles, birds, and mammals), amphibians do not produce what type of eggs?\n\n\nRead this paragraph and choose the correct option from the provided answers:\n\nUnlike other tetrapod vertebrates (reptiles, birds, and mammals), amphibians do not produce amniotic eggs. Therefore, they must lay their eggs in water so they won’t dry out. Their eggs are usually covered in a jelly-like substance, like the frog eggs shown in Figure below . The jelly helps keep the eggs moist and offers some protection from predators.\n\n Choices:\n\n- epithelial\n\n- prokaryotic\n\n- umbilical\n\n- amniotic\n\n\nA:', 'target': 'amniotic', 'rationale': 'The question asks about the type of eggs produced by amphibians and provides three choices. In order to answer this question, you need to understand what an amniotic egg is first. An amniotic egg has a hard shell that protects it from drying out when laid in land instead of water. It also contains fluid filled sacs called membranes:\n- The chorion surrounds the embryo and encloses its own membrane -allantois-, which collects waste products for excretion via specialized structures known as Malpighian tubules (found only in vertebrates). \n- The yolk sac stores food materials until they can be processed by digestive enzymes secreted into the gut after hatching or birth; there are no maternal foods stored inside mammalian ova but other nutrients such as', 'task': 'sciq', 'type': 'CoT'}
