question: 63, answer 151 {'source': "James' father unfortunately had Huntington's disease. This is a disease that can be passed down from generation to generation. His mother Got cancer at one point but it has been treated and has been eliminated.\n\nGiven Huntington;s disease can be passed down, is it a mutation in phenotype or body cells?", 'target': 'phenotype', 'rationale': 'Huntington\'s disease is a genetic condition. It occurs when a single defective gene in your body causes brain cells, called neurons, to waste away. This process of cell death damages areas of the brain that control thinking skills and movement (called cognition and motor function).\nMutations can be present at different levels: \n- DNA mutations alter the sequence of base pairs within genes or chromosomes; this may result in changes to phenotype but it will not cause cancer because no actual physical change has occurred i.e., only the code is altered but nothing else changed physically. \n- Cell mutation refers to alteration in somatic cells which are on other hand become cancerous if mutated causing uncontrollable growth and division leading to tumors or cancers."', 'task': 'ropes', 'type': 'CoT'}
