question: 116, answer 161 {'source': 'The best way to take the pressure off our troops is to succeed in Iraq, is to train Iraqis so they can do the hard work of democracy, is to give them a chance to defend their country, which is precisely what we\'re doing. We\'ll have 125,000 troops trained by the end of this year.\n\nChoose your answer: based on the paragraph above can we conclude that "There doesn\'t need to be pressure taken off our troops in Iraq."?\n\nOPTIONS:\n- Yes\n- It\'s impossible to say\n- No\nI think the answer is', 'target': 'No', 'rationale': 'The paragraph above is a quote from President Bush\'s address to the nation on January 10, 2007. In this speech he discusses his new plan for ending America\'s involvement in Iraq by training Iraqi troops so that they can take over security duties and allow American soldiers to return home while maintaining some presence in the country. The president believes that it will relieve pressure on our troops if we begin executing such a plan since there are currently not enough trained Iraqi soldiers to defend their own country against violent insurgents who oppose democracy and freedom of religion as established after Saddam Hussein was removed from power. Therefore, the answer is "No" because taking pressure off our troops requires them coming home or handing over responsibilities to local forces which would be impossible without success in first stabilizing and then reducing troop levels in Iraq."', 'task': 'anli_r3', 'type': 'CoT'}
