question: 23, answer 157 {'source': 'Q: Sea-lilies, sea cucumbers and sea stars fall into what class of organism?\n\n\nA:', 'target': 'echinoderms', 'rationale': 'Echinoderms are a group of marine organisms characterized by pentamerous radial symmetry, which means that their bodies have five parts. They also have an internal skeleton made up of calcium carbonate plates called ossicles (e.g., sea stars). Sea-lilies and sea cucumbers are actually both echinoderms:\nSea lily is the common name for crinoids or feather stars; they’re considered to be “living fossils" because they first appeared in the Ordovician period ~450 million years ago, but haven\'t changed much since then.\n\n \nThe sea cucumber belongs to a class within Echinodermata called Holothuroidea–they can range from 3 mm - 1 m long with smooth leathery skin', 'task': 'sciq', 'type': 'CoT'}
