question: 96, answer 159 {'source': "I gave my students this multiple choice question: The ocean water near the equator absorbs more heat throughout the year than ocean water near the North Pole. Which of the following best explains this difference?\n\nOnly one answer is correct among these 4 choices:\n- The equator is closer to the Sun.\n- The equator has higher sea levels.\n- The equator receives more direct sunlight.\n- The equator rotates more quickly on Earth's axis.\n\nCould you tell me which one is correct?", 'target': 'The equator receives more direct sunlight.', 'rationale': 'The equator receives more direct sunlight than the water near the North Pole. \nThis is because of the tilt in Earth\'s axis, which means that from June to September or every summer season in one hemisphere (e.g., northern hemisphere), there will be an increase in solar radiation as it reaches its highest point with a higher angle when striking on Earth\'s surface; and for winter months or December through March, due to shorter days and longer nights, that same area would have less solar radiation with lower angles coming into contact with Earth\'s surface.\n\n\nTherefore, given this information provided above: "The ocean water near the equator absorbs more heat throughout the year than ocean water near the North Pole.", The answer should be: "The equator receives more direct sunlight."', 'task': 'ai2_arc@ARC-Challenge', 'type': 'CoT'}
