question: 224, answer 254 {'source': 'What is the next dialog turn? DIALOG:\nWhat is the difference between the Atlantic and Gulf Railroad and other railroads?\n- Construction of the Atlantic and Gulf was forbidden until the junction of the Brunswick and Florida Railroad and the Savannah, Albany, and Gulf Railroad.\n- Are there any other interesting aspects about this article?\n- After that junction had been made the line was to state from the intersection of the county lines of Appling, Ware, and Wayne.\n- Is there anything else significant about the railroad?\n- The borders of those counties have changed substantially since the passage of the act creating the railroad.\n- What did the railroad do?\n- The act authorized the company to extend the route to the western state line at any point between Fort Gaines, Georgia and the confluence of the Flint and Chattahoochee Rivers with the route chosen to allow for speedy access to the Gulf of Mexico through either Pensacola, Florida or Mobile, Alabama.\n- Did the railroad move people or cargo?\n- At that time the Brunswick and Florida had only completed the first of its line.\n- When did the railroad open for business?\n-', 'target': 'On March 31, 1856, the board of commissioners for the Atlantic and Gulf met in Milledgeville to plan for the opening of books and the subscription of stocks.', 'rationale': '"The answer is the fourth option, "On March 31, 1856, the board of commissioners for the Atlantic and Gulf met in Milledgeville to plan for the opening of books and the subscription of stocks." The dialog flow covers a general overview of what happened in each year up to 1856. By this point there have been no questions asked about when anything happened. So we can assume that there will be no questions about time. This leaves us with "What happened?" or "Why did something happen?". The third option ("When did") can be ruled out because it is asking a question that has already been answered (yearly breakdown). The first option ("Why was") doesn\'t seem appropriate because we are given no reason as to why it would be illegal before the junction was made. So this leaves us with two options: "What happened" or "Why did". There seems to be an equal number of both types so far, but since it is not explicitly stated if this pattern should continue I chose not to worry about symmetry; rather I read over all options again and found that one makes more sense than any other - option 4 seems like a logical continuation from where we left off at year 1856."', 'task': 'wiki_dialog', 'type': 'CoT'}
