question: 50, answer 156 {'source': 'Given the fact that:\n\nThe smaller its mass is, the greater its acceleration for a given amount of force.\n\nAnswer the question:\n\n\nWhat happens to a heavy car when it has the same power as a light car accelerates faster or accelerates slower?', 'target': 'accelerates slower', 'rationale': "Acceleration is the rate at which an object's velocity changes over time. In other words, it is how fast something speeds up or slows down. Acceleration depends on two things: mass and force. The greater the mass of an object, the more force required to accelerate that object by a given amount; conversely, if you have a fixed amount of acceleration-producing force but increase an objects mass, its final speed will be lower than before - in other words, it accelerates slower.\nWith this information we can answer our question: since both cars are assumed equal in power (and therefore produce equal amounts of acceleration-producing forces) then the heavier car must require far greater acceleration-inducing forces if they are going to reach their top speeds within similar times as lighter cars with", 'task': 'quartz', 'type': 'CoT'}
