question: 142, answer 156 {'source': 'Given a goal and 2 solutions, choose the most appropriate solution.\nGoal: How can I accurately determine the height distance of a fall or a ledge?\n- Solution 1: Using a glow stick, drop it from the top of the ledge. Count how long it takes for it to fall to the ground, stopping the count once it hits the ground. Each second passed is equal to about 10 meters, or 33 feet.\n- Solution 2: Using a glow stick attached to a rope, drop the glow stick from the ledge and let it fall. Once the glow stick has fallen and reached the ground, pull up the rope and measure the length to determine the height.\n\nAnswer by returning either Solution 1 or Solution 2', 'target': 'Solution 2', 'rationale': 'The goal is to determine the height of a fall or ledge. This can be done using gravity and time, which are both accounted for in Solution 1. In this solution, you drop a glow stick from the top of the ledge and count how long it takes until it touches ground (assuming that there is nothing obstructing its path). The counting provides an approximation of distance because if we assume that each second passed equals about 10 meters, then when multiplied by seconds would give us approximate length in meters – 33 feet per meter. However, even though we could use Solution 1 as our answer here due to its approximated accuracy, based on what was given above with no indication otherwise; I will choose Solution 2 instead since it gives us more accurate results given specific measurements.', 'task': 'piqa', 'type': 'CoT'}
