question: 72, answer 156 {'source': 'I am hesitating between 4 options to answer the following question, which option should I choose?\nQuestion: Humans cannot survive without clean, fresh water. Reservoirs of liquid water vary, but all water spends time as a gas in the atmosphere. What is the source of most of the water in the atmosphere?\nPossibilities:\n- lakes\n- oceans\n- rivers\n- glaciers', 'target': 'oceans', 'rationale': 'Water is a substance that can exist in liquid, solid or gaseous state. The oceans are the principal source of water on Earth and cover about 70% of its surface (Wikipedia). \nThe oceans contain over 90 percent of all the water found on earth, which means that most of the evaporated water from ocean surfaces returns to fall as precipitation into them. Evaporation takes place when liquid changes phase directly into gas. When this happens at atmospheric temperatures and pressures, we call it evaporation; if boiling occurs then vaporization has occurred - Wikipedia) \nIn addition to lakes, rivers also feed into oceans via estuaries/deltas - large bodies where freshwater meets seawater and mixes with each other ("Estuary", NC State University). This process further contributes', 'task': 'ai2_arc@ARC-Easy', 'type': 'CoT'}
