question: 96, answer 158 {'source': 'I am hesitating between 4 options to answer the following question, which option should I choose?\nQuestion: In certain species of plants, purple flowers (P) are dominant to white flowers (p). If two heterozygous plants are crossed, what will be the phenotype of the offspring?\nPossibilities:\n- 100% purple flowers\n- 100% white flowers\n- 75% white flowers, 25% purple flowers\n- 25% white flowers, 75% purple flowers', 'target': '25% white flowers, 75% purple flowers', 'rationale': 'The correct answer is "25% white flowers, 75% purple flowers", which can be determined through the use of Punnett squares. \nIn a first generation cross between two heterozygous parents (Pp x Pp), 25 percent of offspring will have genotype PP and 50 percent will have genotype Pp; there are no pp individuals in this generation. \nBecause dominant alleles always mask recessive ones, all these plants with either PP or Pp genes produce only phenotypically normal purple flowers: none has the recessive phenotype for white flower color.\n\n\nHence, we can conclude that the appropriate choice here would be option D as it correctly states that 25% of progeny have homozygous dominant genotypes while 75 % possess heterozygous', 'task': 'ai2_arc@ARC-Challenge', 'type': 'CoT'}
