question: 119, answer 139 {'source': 'I am hesitating between 4 options to answer the following question, which option should I choose?\nQuestion: How does the tilt of Earth’s axis and its rotation affect the weather?\nPossibilities:\n- The tilt of Earth allows Earth to absorb all of the Sun’s radiation as it rotates.\n- The tilt allows certain latitudes of Earth to be heated at a greater rate while Earth rotates.\n- The tilt of Earth allows Earth to rotate fast enough to allow surface cooling to occur at night.\n- The tilt allows energy to be evenly distributed throughout the atmosphere while Earth rotates.', 'target': 'The tilt allows certain latitudes of Earth to be heated at a greater rate while Earth rotates.', 'rationale': 'The answer is option 2: "The tilt allows certain latitudes of Earth to be heated at a greater rate while Earth rotates."\nHere\'s the explanation why. \nWhen either pole is pointed towards the Sun, that side receives more direct sunlight and experiences summer (and warmer weather), even though it’s actually winter for half of each year. On the other hand, when neither pole points toward or away from the sun, like during an equinox, then every place on earth gets about equal amounts of sunshine regardless of latitude. This means that places around both poles are cold all year round because they don’t get as much heat as countries nearer to the Equator do.', 'task': 'ai2_arc@ARC-Challenge', 'type': 'CoT'}
