question: 134, answer 150 {'source': 'Question: "Where did a mix of Buddhism and Mediterranean culture come about?"\n\nContext: "The gradual spread of Buddhism into adjacent areas meant that it came into contact with new ethnical groups. During this period Buddhism was exposed to a variety of influences, from Persian and Greek civilization, to changing trends in non-Buddhist Indian religions—themselves influenced by Buddhism. Striking examples of this syncretistic development can be seen in the emergence of Greek-speaking Buddhist monarchs in the Indo-Greek Kingdom, and in the development of the Greco-Buddhist art of Gandhāra. A Greek king, Menander, has even been immortalized in the Buddhist canon."\n\nAnswer:', 'target': 'the Indo-Greek Kingdom', 'rationale': 'A mix of Buddhism and Mediterranean culture came about in the Indo-Greek Kingdom, which was established at around 180 BCE by Demetrius I. The kingdom covered eastern Afghanistan, parts of northwestern India (including Gandhara), and small areas to the west. Menander is one such Greek king who ruled part of this kingdom.\nThe choices are: \n[Chi] "Central Asia" - This region comprises territory located between Eastern Europe and Western China as well as northern portions of South Asia. It stretches from the Caspian Sea in the west to China\'s Xinjiang province in the east; its southern boundary varies according to source but ranges from Iran\'s Turkmenistan border down through Afghanistan, Pakistan into Central Asian Republics like Kazakhstan before finally reaching Mongolia with a maritime border', 'task': 'adversarial_qa@dbert', 'type': 'CoT'}
