question: 70, answer 132 {'source': 'I am hesitating between 4 options to answer the following question, which option should I choose?\nQuestion: When a mirror is placed by a fish bowl with a beta fish swimming inside, the beta fish sees what appears to be another fish. This occurs because of\nPossibilities:\n- absorption.\n- refraction.\n- reflection.\n- diffraction.', 'target': 'reflection.', 'rationale': 'The answer is reflection. Reflection occurs when light shines on a smooth surface and bounces off at the same angle that it hit the mirror, causing an image to form behind the mirror. \nA fishbowl acts as a convex lens that causes images of objects in front of it to be reflected back into the bowl. Because mirrors are often placed near bowls with fish inside them, this allows for beta fishes\' brains (which are very small) to see another "fish" without needing eyes facing directly outward from their body. This also helps prevent predators outside of water from seeing movement within the bowl which can cause them to attack or eat the beta fish itself!', 'task': 'ai2_arc@ARC-Challenge', 'type': 'CoT'}
