question: 71, answer 108 {'source': 'Here\'s a short story: A leather glove sliding along a door knob doesn\'t experience as much friction as one sliding along a grab bar. Which surface will let the glove slide a greater distance? (A) door knob (B) grab bar.\n\nWhat is the most sensical answer between "door knob" and  "grab bar"?', 'target': 'door knob', 'rationale': "The force of friction is greater when the two surfaces are rough, as opposed to smooth. A grab bar would typically be made with a rougher surface than that of a door knob because it is used for support and stability, whereas a door knob's primary function is to easily turn on its axis. \nSince grabbing onto something requires more strength than turning knobs, there will be less distance travelled along this surface in comparison to sliding over the smoother one (door knob). Therefore 'Door Knob' makes sense as an answer here.", 'task': 'quarel', 'type': 'CoT'}
