question: 75, answer 166 {'source': 'Mike went to his local grocery store. He noticed that meats had two different packaging, product A and product B. When he looked closely, he found that in product A packaging carbon monoxide was used, but in product B carbon monoxide was not used. He asked the store clerk what difference it made.\n\nWould product A look red longer or shorter than product B?', 'target': 'longer', 'rationale': 'The color red in meat is associated with the presence of oxygen. When carbon monoxide enters the package, it displaces some of the oxygen and makes a small amount of carboxyhemoglobin. This substance has more affinity for myoglobin than does oxymyoglobin so it will "grab" onto available myoglobin molecules to replace any bound oxygen that might be present, thus turning the product from bright cherry-red (oxymyglobin) to deep purplish-brown color(carboxyheme). The difference between A and B would result due to availability of CO which causes removal or binding up of O2 leading to longer shelf life but with long term storage prolonged exposure could lead degradation products like volatile compounds due to lipid oxidation..', 'task': 'ropes', 'type': 'CoT'}
