question: 144, answer 147 {'source': "Answer the following question given this paragraph: \n\nThere are two different types of specific immune responses. One type involves B cells. The other type involves T cells. Recall that B cells and T cells are types of white blood cells that are key in the immune response. Whereas the immune system's first and second line of defense are more generalized or non-specific, the immune response is specific. It can be described as a specific response to a specific pathogen, meaning it uses methods to target just one pathogen at a time. These methods involve B and T cells.\n\n\nQ: B cells and t cells are examples of what type of cells?\n\n Choices:\n\n- heart cells\n\n- cancer cells\n\n- skin cells\n\n- white blood cells\n\nA:", 'target': 'white blood cells', 'rationale': "Among the given options, white blood cells are the only ones included in this paragraph. The first and second line of defense involve non-specific responses to pathogens (i.e., pathogens that have entered a person's body). Non-specific means that it involves general defenses for any pathogen rather than specific defenses against one particular kind of pathogen. When these two lines of defense fail, however, B and T cells trigger an immune response using macromolecules called antibodies or antigens to target specific types of bacteria or viruses; they are examples of specific immunity because they recognize and attack certain kinds of pathogens specifically instead of all kinds generally like in nonspecific immunity \nThe answer is: white blood cells", 'task': 'sciq', 'type': 'CoT'}
