question: 136, answer 163 {'source': "Here's a problem to solve: A hydrologist is studying the acidity level of a city lake. She collects samples twice a week and takes them to her lab to test them. After testing the samples, she places the data on a graph. She tells the city engineers that her tests indicate that the pH level has been steadily dropping over the past few months. Which of the following steps is the best way to check the accuracy of her data?\n\nAmong the 4 following options, which is the correct answer?\n\n- A: retest new samples\n \n- B: retest the original samples\n \n- C: show the graphs to the engineers\n \n- D: show the samples to the engineers", 'target': 'B', 'rationale': 'The best way to check the accuracy of her data is by retesting the original samples. \nOption A, "retest new samples" will not help because it would be impossible for all other factors that might affect pH levels in a lake (such as temperature and weather) to remain exactly constant throughout both runs of tests. This could introduce inaccuracy into the results despite following proper testing procedure during both test periods. Option B, "retest the original samples", however, can eliminate this possibility since there are no changes between time periods or sample sets and thus no variables could have influenced either set of results differently. Option C, "show graphs to engineers" only shows them trends but does not reveal any details about how accurate those trends are due to possible inaccuracies in recording or representing data', 'task': 'ai2_arc@ARC-Challenge', 'type': 'CoT'}
