question: 134, answer 177 {'source': "Given a real-life anecdote of a complex ethical situation, identify who is wrong here. Classify each post into the following categories : AUTHOR (if the author of the post is wrong in the situation), OTHER (if any of the others mentioned in the paragraph is wrong), EVERYBODY (if everyone mentioned is wrong) and NOBODY (if no one is wrong in the situation).\n\nThey're pretty strong in English, and my friend is fluent. Right now I'm just sitting at the table smiling and nodding while it's 90% Arabic. I would never actually ask this though, or I'd seem rude and unaccepting.", 'target': 'NOBODY', 'rationale': 'The author is at a meal with friends. The friends are speaking in Arabic and the author is not fluent in Arabic, but one of the friends is fluent in English. Despite not understanding what they are saying, the author sits at the table smiling and nodding while they speak. The author\'s friend could translate what they are saying so that everyone can participate equally, but it seems there has been no attempt to do so on their part.\\nIn this situation, no one is acting inappropriately towards anyone else because each person has an opportunity to participate equally and yet nobody takes that opportunity. There also appears to be no intention of being rude or unaccepting on any side; rather it seems as though nobody wishes to change their current behavior enough for things to change. Therefore, no one is wrong here so \'NOBODY\' would be a correct choice for this post."', 'task': 'scruples', 'type': 'CoT'}
