question: 31, answer 156 {'source': 'Q: What is a decrease in the magnitude of the membrane potential?\n\n\n Choices:\n\n- digestion\n\n- ionization\n\n- inflammation\n\n- depolarization\n\nA:', 'target': 'depolarization', 'rationale': 'The simplest way to answer this question is elimination.\nDigestion and inflammation are not related to the topic of membrane potential, so we can eliminate those options right off the bat! That just leaves ionization and depolarization. Ionization means “the process by which a stable neutral atom loses or gains one electron” (https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/ionize). In terms of membranes, ion channels allow ions like Na+ and Ca2+ across the plasma membrane in order to maintain homeostasis within cells; thus, if there was an increase in these types of ions moving through a cell wall it would result in increased electrical activity – NOT decreased as called for by our definition: decrease in magnitude = less energy than before', 'task': 'sciq', 'type': 'CoT'}
