question: 26, answer 118 {'source': 'Q: Insects can have specialized mouthparts, such as a proboscis, to siphon the nectar from what?\n\n\nA:', 'target': 'flowers', 'rationale': 'Insects use specialized mouthparts to siphon nectar from flowers. The question specifically asks about the insect’s proboscis, which is an elongated tubular structure that insects such as bees and butterflies possess for imbibing liquids like nectar. Because of its special shape, a proboscis can fit into long narrow tubes found in some types of flowers. This makes it easier for insects with this type of mouthpart to feed on plants by sucking up their sweet juices or other fluids through these structures (i.e., floral tubes). So the answer is “flowers."', 'task': 'sciq', 'type': 'CoT'}
