question: 96, answer 118 {'source': "Here's a problem to solve: European starlings are birds that take over the nests of other birds. When they move into an area, starlings reproduce quickly. Taking over the nests of other birds most likely increases the number of starlings by helping them to do what?\n\nAmong the 4 following options, which is the correct answer?\n\n- A: attract a better mate\n \n- B: compete for resources\n \n- C: migrate during the winter\n \n- D: blend in with the environment", 'target': 'B', 'rationale': 'The correct answer is B \nWhen European starlings take over the nests of other birds, it most likely helps them to compete for resources. This is because when they move into an area, where other species are already established, these birds will have limited access to food and shelter in comparison with those that live there before them. As a result, this would cause more competition among animals within each species as well as between different bird species living nearby. The increase in competition due to the arrival of new members (starling) may affect all individuals negatively since it makes survival harder than before their presence.', 'task': 'ai2_arc@ARC-Easy', 'type': 'CoT'}
