question: 31, answer 159 {'source': 'Q: What is the touch response in plants called?\n\n\n Choices:\n\n- phototropism\n\n- pollenation\n\n- sensitivity\n\n- thigmotropism\n\nA:', 'target': 'thigmotropism', 'rationale': 'The question asks what the touch response in plants is called. The given choices are phototropism, pollenation, sensitivity and thigmotropism.\nWhen a plant touches or has something touching it, the part of that plant closest to where it was touched will bend away from whatever is touching it because its cells grow faster on one side than the other. This makes sense for many reasons including: \n- limiting damage due to physical contact with an object (for example when wind sweeps through tall grass)\n- helping vines climb by aligning them upwards towards sunlight and gravity\n\n Hence we can conclude that plants respond to physical contact by bending away from objects which have been identified as having damaged their cell walls; this phenomenon is known as "thigmotropism". So thigmotropism', 'task': 'sciq', 'type': 'CoT'}
