question: 28, answer 146 {'source': 'Q: What is used to record seismic waves?\n\n\n Choices:\n\n- transmitter\n\n- odometer\n\n- topometer\n\n- seismograph\n\nA:', 'target': 'seismograph', 'rationale': 'A seismograph is an instrument that records the vibrations of seismic waves. Seismic waves are created by earthquakes and volcanic eruptions, among other things. The general term for these kinds of natural events is called tectonic activity; it refers to any shifting or movement in Earth’s crust as a result of plate movements and interactions with magma beneath the surface.\n\nSo, because we know what seismic waves are caused by, it makes sense that their measurements need to be recorded somehow—this is where the seismograph comes into play: not only does this device record seismic wave vibrations but also measures them using mechanical-electrical means (i.e., levers).\n\n \nThe answer is “seismograph."', 'task': 'sciq', 'type': 'CoT'}
