question: 106, answer 157 {'source': 'Q: What is the term for rain that has a ph less than 5, due to carbon dioxide dissolving?\n\n\nRead this paragraph and choose the correct option from the provided answers:\n\nAcid rain is rain that has a pH less than 5 (see Figure below ). The pH of normal rain is 5.6. It’s slightly acidic because carbon dioxide in the air dissolves in rain. This forms carbonic acid, a weak acid.\n\n Choices:\n\n- frozen rain\n\n- basic rain\n\n- carbon rain\n\n- acid rain\n\n\nA:', 'target': 'acid rain', 'rationale': 'Acid rain is a broad term that describes any precipitation with pH levels below 5.6, the average acidity of rainfall on Earth (see Figure above). The primary cause of acid rain is sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides released into the atmosphere when fossil fuels are burned at power plants or factories. Acid rain can also result from other sources like natural gases in swamps, geysers, and volcanoes. \nAccording to the paragraph provided: "The pH of normal rain is 5.6." So this means it\'s slightly acidic because carbon dioxide dissolves in rain forming carbonic acid which makes weak acids thus making it an \'acid\' rather than basic as others would assume but we do not have enough information about frozen or carbon rains so they make no sense but according to our', 'task': 'sciq', 'type': 'CoT'}
