question: 144, answer 162 {'source': "Given the background: Fish reproduce sexually. They lay eggs that can be fertilized either inside or outside of the body. In most fish, the eggs develop outside of the mother's body. In the majority of these species, fertilization also takes place outside the mother's body. The male and female fish release their gametes into the surrounding water, where fertilization occurs. Female fish release very high numbers of eggs to increase the chances of fertilization.\n\nand the situation: Tammy and Moe were two school mates. Moe learned about mammalian reproduction in school. Tammy learned about fish reproduction.\n\nAnswer the following question: Which student learned about fish eggs being fertilized inside of the female body?", 'target': 'Tammy', 'rationale': "Although many species of fish reproduce by releasing their gametes into the surrounding water, some do not. Some sharks and rays are ovoviviparous; they give birth to live young through eggs that develop inside of the mother's body. In this way, fertilization occurs internally for these fish species.\nGiven that Tammy learned about fish reproduction in school but Moe did not learn about mammalian reproduction, it can be inferred that Tammy is learning about all aspects of sexual reproduction in a general sense while Moe is focusing on mammals specifically (which includes humans). \nThus, out of the two students given Tammy would have been more likely to know about internal fertilization within female fishes' bodies as compared with Moe who was studying human reproductive biology only and wouldn't have known such details from his", 'task': 'ropes', 'type': 'CoT'}
