question: 96, answer 152 {'source': "Pick the most correct option to answer the following question.\n\nA research scientist repeatedly observes a bird avoiding a specific butterfly species even  though it eats other types of butterflies. Which statement most likely explains the behavior of the bird?\n\nOptions:\n\n- A: The behavior is a random act.\n\n- B: The behavior is the result of a genetic mutation.\n\n- C: The behavior is inherited from the bird's parents.\n\n- D: The behavior is learned over the lifetime of the bird.", 'target': 'D', 'rationale': 'The question asks for the most correct explanation of why a bird avoids one type of butterfly. \nIf this behavior were inherited from its parents or caused by a random act, it is unlikely that the scientist would be able to observe it repeatedly over time. The same applies if the behavior were genetic mutation: mutations are typically observed once and not repeated consistently across individuals. By contrast, behaviors may vary widely when they have been learned rather than being inherited; thus, an individual might show them again and again in contrasting circumstances (although learning can also produce consistent patterns). \nTherefore, option D seems to indicate that birds learn their avoidance behaviors throughout their lifetimes - which explains why a researcher could continue observing such repetition without necessarily concluding that there was any hereditary basis for those behaviors.', 'task': 'ai2_arc@ARC-Challenge', 'type': 'CoT'}
