question: 91, answer 138 {'source': 'Answer the question below:\n\n\nA dark skinned man and a light skinned man are being tested to see who produces vitamin D faster. The light skinned person will have vitamin D in amounts that are greater or lesser? \n\n\nAssuming that:\n\nPigments in the skin act like a filter that can prevent the skin from making vitamin D. As a result, people with darker skin need more time in the sun than people with lighter skin to make the same amount of vitamin D.', 'target': 'greater', 'rationale': 'It is mentioned that pigment in the skin acts like a filter and can prevent vitamin D from being produced. As such, people with darker skin take longer to produce the same amount of vitamin D than someone with lighter skin. So since we want to compare two men who are different colors but will be producing equal amounts of Vitamin D, it must be true then that at some point during testing this dark-skinned man was producing more Vitamin D per unit time because he had been out in the sun for less time (due to his pigmentation) than his light-skinned counterpart - even though they both produced equal amounts by the end of testing. Thus, we choose greater as our answer here!', 'task': 'quartz', 'type': 'CoT'}
