question: 47, answer 161 {'source': 'Q: Because the human body is about 70% water by mass, and liquid water has high specific heat, a great deal of what is required to change its temperature?\n\n\n Choices:\n\n- power\n\n- work\n\n- digestion\n\n- energy\n\nA:', 'target': 'energy', 'rationale': 'Specific heat is the amount of energy required to change one gram of a substance by 1 degree Celsius. Water has a high specific heat, meaning that it takes more energy (heat) than other substances to raise its temperature by an equivalent amount. This means that water is great at regulating temperatures because it can absorb large amounts of heat without changing very much in temperature itself; this also means that under normal circumstances, liquid water cannot boil and freeze simultaneously since both processes require extreme changes in temperature and would cancel each other out. Since humans are primarily made up of water, they too need significant amounts of thermal energy for physical transformations such as boiling or freezing due to their high concentration of H2O molecules which share similar properties with pure liquid water (in fact, blood plasma consists mostly of dissolved salts suspended within', 'task': 'sciq', 'type': 'CoT'}
